# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Upper Strategy — English reading."""

TITLE = 'Book One — The Upper Strategy'
HEAD = '''Sixty-four passages, and the whole book is a book of quotation. The <i>Upper Strategy</i> cites the <i>Military Prognostications</i> nineteen times, and most of what it says in its own voice is commentary on what it has just quoted — so to read the <i>Upper Strategy</i> is to read the surviving fragments of a lost book. The <i>Military Prognostications</i> is in no imperial catalogue; nineteen passages quoted here are all of it that is left. “The soft can subdue the hard, the weak can subdue the strong” is one of them, and so is “under a fragrant bait there is always a dead fish; under a heavy reward there is always a brave man.” The opening sentence states the programme without preamble: <i>take hold of the hearts of the heroes</i>. The closing pages list ten marks of a state about to fall — the ruined state, the state’s traitors, the source of disorder, the root of disorder — and not one of them is about how to fight. The Qing imperial catalogue thought the book “shallow in sense, unlike an ancient book,” and Zheng Yuan called it warmed-over Laozi, “slow and rambling, of no practical use.” Against that stands a fact: an edict of Emperor Guangwu of AD 51, preserved in the biography of Zang Gong in the <i>History of the Later Han</i>, quotes this book at length and by the name <i>Record of the Yellow Stone Elder</i> — not only “the soft can subdue the hard, the weak can subdue the strong,” but three consecutive passages of what is now the <i>Lower Strategy</i>. Both cases have evidence behind them; the colophon sets out what there is.'''

PARA = {
 'j1-1': 'The method of the commander lies in taking hold of the hearts of the heroes: salary those who have earned it, and let your purpose reach the whole body of your men. Share what the multitude likes and nothing you attempt will fail; share what the multitude hates and nothing you attack will stand. A state governed and a house at peace mean you have won men; a state lost and a house broken mean you have lost them. Every creature that draws breath wants to attain what it wants. (The book opens on <i>winning men</i>, not on waging war, and never leaves that ground.)',
 'j1-2': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “The soft can subdue the hard, the weak can subdue the strong.” The soft is virtue; the hard is injury. The weak is what men will help; the strong is what resentment attacks. (The <i>Military Prognostications</i> is a lost work; nineteen passages quoted in this book are all that survives. This is the most famous of them — Emperor Guangwu’s edict of AD 51 quotes it by name. In the last clause the Siku plain text and Liu Yin’s commentary both read “the strong is what <i>men</i> attack,” making an exact antithesis with the line before. The 1604 Fushimi printing has “resentment” in its text, but carries a later hand’s marginal note on the opening leaf — “the <i>Exposition</i> has <i>resentment</i>, the <i>Direct Explanation</i> has <i>men</i>” — so a Japanese reader had already recorded this variant four hundred years ago. <b>And the edict of AD 51 goes straight on: “the weak is what humaneness helps, the strong is what <i>resentment</i> settles upon”</b> — the first-century quotation has the same word. The base text’s reading is kept.)',
 'j1-3': 'There is a place to set out softness and a place to apply hardness; a use for weakness and an occasion to add strength. Hold all four, and judge which the case requires. (This is the corrective to the passage before: softness is not a policy of softness. The sentence the whole passage rests on is the last one.)',
 'j1-4': 'When neither end is shown, no one can know you. Be like heaven, earth and the spirits, moving with things; be without constant form, changing as the enemy changes. Do not act first; move only in answer. So you can plan without limit and help heaven’s authority to its work, settling the eight directions and quietly pacifying the nine tribes. A planner of this kind is teacher to emperors and kings. (Laozi’s refusal to take the lead, put to military use. This is the sort of passage Zheng Yuan meant when he called the book warmed-over Laozi.)',
 'j1-5': 'Hence the saying: “Everyone covets strength; few can hold to the small. He who can hold to the small keeps his life.',
 'j1-6': 'The sage keeps this in mind and meets events with it. Unrolled, it fills the four seas; rolled up, it does not fill a cup. It needs no house to be lodged in and no city wall to be guarded; hidden in the breast, it makes enemy states submit.” (Great enough to have no outside, small enough to have no inside. The base text runs this and the previous passage together; the division here follows Liu Yin’s, so a continuation quotation mark is supplied at the head of the second, in the ordinary way.)',
 'j1-7': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “The state that can be soft and also hard grows ever brighter; the state that can be weak and also strong grows ever more distinguished. A state purely soft and weak will be pared away; a state purely hard and strong will perish.” (The word doing the work is <i>purely</i>.)',
 'j1-8': 'The way of governing a state rests on the worthy and on the people. Trust the worthy as your own heart and use the people as your own limbs, and no plan will go astray. (The base text has a graph meaning “sent away” where both witnesses have “lost, gone astray”; the two are near-identical in form and the reading is corrected here.)',
 'j1-9': 'Then wherever you go, it is as limbs following one another and joints coming to one another’s aid — the way of heaven itself, and so deft that there is no seam in it.',
 'j1-10': 'The essential thing in army and state is to read the mind of the multitude, and then to set the hundred businesses in motion. (What follows is the most nearly regulation-like passage in the book. Liu Yin cut it into six sections and this page follows him.)',
 'j1-11': 'Those in danger — settle them. Those in fear — make them glad. Those who have defected — bring them back. Those with a grievance — allow for it. Those who bring complaint — look into it. Those of low station — make them honoured. The overbearing — hold them down. Those set against you — cut them off. (For “those with a grievance” both witnesses read “those wronged,” which runs on better into the next clause.)',
 'j1-12': 'The greedy — let them have their fill. The ambitious — set them to work. The timid — cover for them. The able planner — keep him close.',
 'j1-13': 'The slanderer — check what he says. The defamed — restore his name. The rebellious — remove him. The unruly — break him.',
 'j1-14': 'The self-satisfied — take him down a degree. Those who come over — welcome them. Those who submit — settle them. Those who surrender — release them. (For “settle them” both witnesses read “spare their lives.”)',
 'j1-15': 'Take a stronghold and hold it; take a defile and block it; take a hard place and garrison it; take a city and divide it up; take land and parcel it out; take goods and scatter them among the men. If the enemy moves, watch him; if he draws near, guard against him; if he is strong, put yourself below him; if he is at ease, leave him alone; if he presses, wait him out; if he is brutal, soothe him; if he is perverse, arraign him in the name of right; if he is united, set his people against one another. (Divide, parcel, scatter — nothing is kept. The next passage says as much outright.)',
 'j1-16': 'If he raises troops with the current, thwart him; where there is momentum to be used, break him with it; if he puts words abroad, let them pass; and when the net is spread on four sides, gather him in.',
 'j1-17': 'Having got it, do not own it. Having occupied it, do not garrison it. Having stormed it, do not stay. Having set someone up, do not take his place.',
 'j1-18': 'The doing is yours, the having is your officers’; who is to say where the profit lies? Let others be feudal lords, and be the Son of Heaven yourself. Let the cities look to their own defence and the officers to their own places. (A cold paragraph, and a deliberate one.)',
 'j1-19': 'The world can honour its ancestors; few can stoop to those below. To honour ancestors is what makes a kinsman; to stoop to those below is what makes a ruler.',
 'j1-20': 'To stoop to those below is to keep them at plough and loom and not rob them of the season; to tax lightly and not drain their means; to levy labour sparingly and not wear them out. Then the state is rich and households are content, and only then do you choose officers to shepherd them.',
 'j1-21': 'What is meant by “officers” is the heroes. Hence: net the heroes and the enemy state is impoverished.',
 'j1-22': 'The heroes are the trunk of the state; the common people are its root. Get the trunk, gather in the root, and government runs without complaint.',
 'j1-23': 'The essential thing in using troops is to hold ceremony high and salary heavy. Ceremony held high brings men of intelligence; salary held heavy makes men of principle careless of death. So do not grudge money in salarying the worthy, and do not let the hour pass in rewarding merit: then the strength below you is joined in one and the enemy state is worn down.',
 'j1-24': 'The way of employing men: honour them with rank and let them see wealth, and officers come of themselves; receive them with ceremony and urge them with duty, and officers will die for you. (For “let them see wealth” both witnesses read “provide for them with wealth.”)',
 'j1-25': 'A general must eat the same food as his men and share the same danger before he can take the enemy on; then his army has whole victory and the enemy is wholly at his disposal.',
 'j1-26': 'Once there was a good general to whom a jar of wine was given. He had it poured into the river and drank from the stream with his men. One jar of wine cannot flavour a river; yet the men of the three armies wanted to die for him, because that taste had after all reached them. (The base text omits the word “troops” after “used”; both witnesses have it, and it is supplied.)',
 'j1-27': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “Until the army’s well is sunk, the general does not speak of thirst. Until the army’s tents are pitched, the general does not speak of weariness. Until the army’s stove is lit, the general does not speak of hunger. In winter he wears no fur, in summer he plies no fan, in rain he raises no canopy.” This is called a general’s courtesy. (For “stove” the base text has the simplified form; both witnesses have the older one, restored here.)',
 'j1-28': 'He is with them in safety and with them in danger. So his men can be joined and cannot be parted, can be used and cannot be worn out — because the kindness was laid down beforehand and the thinking was agreed beforehand. Hence: never tire of laying down kindness, and take ten thousand with one.',
 'j1-29': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “What gives a general his authority is his orders. What makes a battle a whole victory is the discipline of the army. What makes the men careless of death is that they are under command. So the general issues no order he must call back, and keeps faith in reward and punishment as heaven and earth keep faith: then he can use men, and when the men are under command he may cross the border.” (For “careless of death” both witnesses read “careless of battle.”)',
 'j1-30': 'It is the general who commands the army and holds the advantage; it is the multitude who wins the victory and beats the enemy. So a disordered general must not be given an army to keep, and a disaffected multitude must not be sent to attack. The city is not stormed, the town not reduced. Both come to nothing, and then the strength of the men is spent; the strength spent, the general is isolated and the multitude is at odds; in defence nothing holds, in battle they run. This is called a stale army. (Where the base text has a graph meaning “ride,” which makes no sense, both witnesses have one meaning “at odds, disaffected,” answering “disordered general” in the clause before; the reading is corrected here.)',
 'j1-31': 'An army gone stale, and the general’s authority will not run. The general without authority, and the men make light of punishment. The men making light of punishment, and the ranks break. The ranks broken, and the men desert. The men deserting, and the enemy has his opening. The enemy with his opening, and the army is destroyed. (Seven stages, each hooked to the last — the book’s favourite figure.)',
 'j1-32': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “A good general commands his army as leniently as he treats himself; he pushes the benefit outward and lets the kindness down, and the strength of his men is new every day. In battle they break out like wind; in attack they go like a river through a dyke.” So his men can be seen and cannot be withstood, can be submitted to and cannot be beaten. Because he goes ahead of them himself, his troops are the finest under heaven.',
 'j1-33': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “An army has reward for its outer side and punishment for its lining.” When reward and punishment are clear, the general’s authority runs. When officials are well chosen, the men obey. When the worthy are employed, enemy states are afraid. (The base text’s “wrapping” is unintelligible; both witnesses have “lining,” which pairs with “outer side.”)',
 'j1-34': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “Where a worthy man goes, nothing stands before him. So an officer may be deferred to but not treated with arrogance; a general may be made glad but must not be left anxious; a plan may be deep but must not be doubted.”',
 'j1-35': 'Let officers grow arrogant and those under them stop obeying; let the general be anxious and inside and outside cease to trust one another; let the plan be doubted and the enemy state takes heart. March out to attack in that condition and you will only bring on disorder.',
 'j1-36': 'The general is the life of the state. If the general can win, the state is secure.',
 'j1-37': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “A general must be able to be clear, to be still, to be even-handed, to be orderly; able to take remonstrance, to hear a suit, to receive men, to take up what is said; able to know the customs of a country, to map its rivers and hills, to mark its hard passages, and to hold military authority.” (Twelve times “able.”)',
 'j1-38': 'Hence: “The intelligence of the humane and worthy, the deliberation of the sage, the words of the man carrying firewood, the talk of the great hall, the history of rises and falls — all of these a general ought to hear.” A general who thinks of men of ability the way a thirsty man thinks of water will find that plans come to him. (“The man carrying firewood” is the peasant; “the great hall” is the court. High and low set side by side.)',
 'j1-39': 'If the general refuses remonstrance, the heroes disperse; if his plans are not followed, the planners defect; if good and bad count the same, men of merit grow slack; if he goes his own way, blame is left at his door; if he vaunts himself, few below him will achieve anything; if he believes slanderers, hearts turn away; if he is greedy for money, corruption cannot be stopped; if his mind is on his household, the men grow licentious.',
 'j1-40': 'One of these, and the multitude will not obey. Two, and the army has no standard. Three, and those below him run. Four, and the calamity reaches the state.',
 'j1-41': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “Let the general’s planning be close, the men’s minds be one, and the attack be quick.” Planning close, and treacherous hearts are shut out. Minds one, and the will of the army holds together. Attack quick, and the enemy’s preparations cannot be laid. An army with these three cannot have its plan taken from it.',
 'j1-42': 'Let the general’s plan leak and the army has no momentum. Let the outside see the inside and the calamity cannot be checked. Let money into the camp and every sort of treachery gathers. A general with these three will certainly be beaten.',
 'j1-43': 'A general without forethought, and the planners leave. A general without courage, and the men are afraid. A general who acts rashly, and the army has no weight. A general who passes on his anger, and the whole army is in fear.',
 'j1-44': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “Forethought and courage are what a general values; action and anger are what he employs. These four are a general’s plain warning.” (The base text has a graph that is a copyist’s corruption of “plain, clear”; both witnesses have the right one. Forethought and courage are what he is; action and anger are what he does with it — and doing it badly produces the faults of the passage above.)',
 'j1-45': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “Where an army has no money, officers do not come; where an army has no reward, officers do not go.”',
 'j1-46': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “Under a fragrant bait there is always a dead fish; under a heavy reward there is always a brave man.” (The most widely quoted sentence in the book. It is not a compliment: the dead fish is the other half of it.)',
 'j1-47': 'So ceremony is what officers come home to, and reward is what they die for. Hold out what they come home to and show them what they die for, and the man you are looking for arrives.',
 'j1-48': 'But if you show ceremony and then regret it, officers do not stay; if you promise reward and then regret it, officers cannot be used. Never tire of ceremony and reward, and officers will compete to die.',
 'j1-49': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “A state about to raise troops first deepens its kindness; a state about to attack and take first feeds its people.” To beat many with few is a matter of kindness; to beat the strong with the weak is a matter of the people.',
 'j1-50': 'So a good general keeps his officers no worse than he keeps himself, and can make three armies of one mind, and the victory is whole.',
 'j1-51': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “The essential thing in using troops is first to make out the enemy’s condition: look at his stores, measure his grain, divine his strength and weakness, observe his season and ground, and watch for his gaps.”',
 'j1-52': 'So a state with no military emergency that is yet moving grain is a state run dry; people with the colour of weeds in their faces are a people in want. Grain carried a thousand li, and there is hunger in the soldiers’ faces; firewood cut and grass gathered before the fire is lit, and the army never has a full night’s meal.',
 'j1-53': 'Carry grain a thousand li and there is not a year’s food; two thousand, not two years’; three thousand, not three years’ — this is called an empty state. The state empty, and the people are poor; the people poor, and high and low are not close. The enemy attacking outside and the people robbing inside: this is called certain collapse. (For “two thousand” and “three thousand” Liu Yin’s text has “two hundred” and “three hundred,” as does the Fushimi printing, which also carries a later hand’s small “hundred” beside the first figure. The Siku plain text agrees with the base. Three witnesses, two readings; the base is kept.)',
 'j1-54': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “When those above act cruelly, those below turn harsh; when taxes are heavy and levies frequent and punishments have no limit, the people prey on one another — this is called a ruined state.” (Ten passages follow, each naming a symptom of a state about to fall. None of them is about war.)',
 'j1-55': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “Greedy within and clean without; taking a name by false repute; making the public purse into private favour so that high and low are confused; grooming the person and composing the face to get high office — this is called the opening for theft.”',
 'j1-56': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “When the officials form factions and each promotes his own; when the crooked are recommended and the humane and worthy held down; when the public is turned aside for the private and colleagues sneer at one another — this is called the source of disorder.”',
 'j1-57': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “When powerful clans gather the treacherous and men without office are honoured; when a man has weight and is not cowed and they hang together like creeper on creeper; when private favour is planted and the power of those in office taken away; when the people below are abused until the country is in uproar and the ministers dare not speak — this is called the root of disorder.”',
 'j1-58': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “When generation after generation practises fraud, embezzles from the public office, advances and retreats only for its own convenience, and twists the wording of documents to endanger its lord — this is called the state’s traitors.”',
 'j1-59': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “When officials are many and the people few; when high and low are barely distinguishable and strong and weak plunder one another; when no one is fit to restrain them, so that even the good are drawn in — the state pays for it.”',
 'j1-60': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “When the good are not advanced and the bad are not removed; when the worthy are in hiding and the unfit are in office — the state suffers for it.”',
 'j1-61': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “When the branches grow great and hold the ground by faction; when the base ride over the honourable and grow greater with time; when those above cannot bring themselves to cut them off — the state comes to grief by it.”',
 'j1-62': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “When a flatterer is at the top and the whole army is in dispute; when he draws authority to himself and every move of his goes against the many; when he neither advances nor retreats but only keeps himself agreeable; when he does everything himself and every act is a claim to credit; when he slanders the virtuous and talks up nonentities; when good and bad are all one so long as they agree with him; when business is held up and orders do not get through; when harsh regulations are invented and the old ways changed — a lord who employs a flatterer will certainly suffer for it.” (The longest passage in the book: sixteen clauses.)',
 'j1-63': 'The <i>Military Prognostications</i> says: “When the treacherous praise one another and so screen the ruler’s sight; when blame and praise rise together and so stop the ruler’s hearing; when each takes the side of his own — so the ruler is made to lose his loyal men.”',
 'j1-64': 'So when the ruler hears a dissenting word, he looks for what it is growing from. When the ruler engages scholars and worthies, the treacherous depart. When he employs men of long standing, ten thousand matters fall into order. When he calls men out of their caves, officers give him the truth. When he takes counsel with the woodcutter, the work is worth recording. Let him not lose the hearts of men, and his virtue spreads and increases. (The book’s first sentence was “take hold of the hearts of the heroes”; the scroll closes on “let him not lose the hearts of men.”)',
}
